    "price = df['price']\n",
    "\n",
    "Q1 = price.quantile(0.25)\n",
    "Q3 = price.quantile(0.75)\n",
    "IQR = Q3 - Q1\n",
    "iqr_mask = (price < Q1 - 1.5 * IQR) | (price > Q3 + 1.5 * IQR)\n",
    "print(f\"Outliers IQR : {iqr_mask.sum()}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "median = price.median()\n",
    "mad = (price - median).abs().median()\n",
    "modified_z = 0.6745 * (price - median) / mad\n",
    "mad_mask = abs(modified_z) > 3.5\n",
    "print(f\"Outliers modified Z-score : {mad_mask.sum()}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "low = price.quantile(0.01)\n",
    "high = price.quantile(0.99)\n",
    "pct_mask = (price < low) | (price > high)\n",
    "print(f\"Outliers percentiles : {pct_mask.sum()}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "outliers_combined = df[iqr_mask & mad_mask & pct_mask]\n",
    "print(f\"Outliers in the 3 methods : {len(outliers_combined)}\")\n",
    "outliers_combined[['price','sqm_living','grade','bedrooms']].describe()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2204c008",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Decision: we keep the 217 price outliers.**\n",
    "\n",
    "- These are the houses flagged by all 3 methods at once: prices from 1.96 M$ to over 7 M$, large areas (463 m² on average) and a high grade (7 to 13). They are consistent with each other: they are real luxury homes, not errors.\n",
    "- Removing them would remove the whole top of the market: the model would be unable to predict these properties.\n",
    "- To limit their weight in the models, we will use `log(price)` as the target during modeling, and use models that are not very sensitive to extreme values (Random Forest, Gradient Boosting).\n",
    "- The price stays in its original value in the cleaned file (the log is a modeling step, not a cleaning step)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "be8bba8f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Standardization: not in this notebook.**\n",
    "\n",
    "Standardization (`StandardScaler`) will be done inside the modeling pipeline, after the train / test split: the mean and standard deviation are computed on the train set only, otherwise information from the test set would leak into the training. It only concerns models sensitive to scale (SVR). `zipcode` will be treated as a categorical variable (One-Hot Encoding).\n",
    "\n",
    "**To exclude from modeling:** `sqm_price_per_lot` and `sqm_price_per_living`. They are computed from the price and would give the answer to the model. They stay in the cleaned file for the exploratory analysis (Power BI)."
